class TernaryTreeEncoding:
    r"""
    A ternary tree compiled into the Pauli images of its Majorana operators.
    
    Pairing the tree's legs into Majorana operators and building their Pauli strings is done once,
    here, rather than per mapped operator. Pass one of these to the ternary-tree mappers.
    
    The pairing follows Algorithm 1 of the Bonsai paper [1]_: from each node, descend its ``X`` link
    and then ``Z`` links until reaching a leg, which carries :math:`\gamma` of that node's mode; the
    ``Y`` link likewise gives :math:`\gamma'`. This is what makes :math:`|0\cdots0\rangle` the
    fermionic vacuum, so it is not an adjustable convention.
    
    .. [1] A. Miller, Z. Zimborás, S. Knecht, S. Maniscalco and G. García-Pérez, Bonsai algorithm:
           grow your own fermion-to-qubit mappings, PRX Quantum 4, 030314 (2023),
           `arXiv:2212.09731 <https://arxiv.org/abs/2212.09731>`_.
    
    Usage
    =====
    
    .. doctest::
    
        >>> from qiskit_fermions.mappers import TernaryTree, TernaryTreeEncoding
        >>> enc = TernaryTreeEncoding(TernaryTree.breadth_first(4, 3))
        >>> enc.num_modes, enc.max_weight
        (4, 2)
    
    Two encodings compare equal when they assign the same Pauli string to every Majorana operator,
    which is what lets a set or dictionary deduplicate them. Leaving out the mode map is the same as
    giving the identity, whereas any other one changes which Majorana operator each string belongs
    to:
    
    .. doctest::
    
        >>> tree = TernaryTree.chain(3, "Z")
        >>> TernaryTreeEncoding(tree) == TernaryTreeEncoding(tree, [0, 1, 2])
        True
        >>> TernaryTreeEncoding(tree) == TernaryTreeEncoding(tree, [2, 1, 0])
        False
    
    The encoding is built once and reused across every operator mapped through it, so the pairing
    and string construction are not repeated per call. The qubit count of a mapped operator is given
    to the mapper rather than fixed here, so one encoding serves registers of any width from
    :attr:`num_modes` upwards.
    
    An encoding cannot be pickled. It is a derived artifact: compiling discards the tree and the
    mode map it was built from, so supporting pickle would mean retaining them purely to serialize
    them. Pickle the :class:`TernaryTree` (and the mode map) and compile again instead.
    """
